    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from the inside to the outside. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes, changing between liquid and gas. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it travels back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over. Because heat keeps getting carried away, the inside stays cold, keeping your food fresh and safe."
